\begin{abstract}
We prove, in complete generality and with explicit constructions, that no finite prefix of the
decimal expansion of a real number constrains the asymptotic behaviour of that expansion.
Precisely: for every real $x \ge 0$ and every $n \in \NN$ there exist real numbers $y$, $z$, $w$
agreeing with $x$ in the first $n$ decimal digits such that $y$ is irrational with
nonzero-digit density $0$, $z$ is irrational with nonzero-digit density $1$, $w$ is rational,
and none of the three is simply normal in base ten. The same prefix therefore decides neither
rationality, nor any digit frequency, nor normality. We prove a companion statement for the
second-order statistic: for every $x$ and $n$ there are irrational numbers sharing the first
$n$ digits of $x$ whose lag-$r$ digit agreement densities are, respectively, $0$ at lag $1$ and
$1$ at lag $2$, or $1$ at every lag $r$ simultaneously. We further show that the witness set is
uncountable for every prefix, that every nonnegative real is an $\varepsilon$-limit of both such
irrational witnesses and of rationals with the same prefix, and---on the positive side---that
irrationality has exactly one digit-theoretic consequence: for $x \in [0,1)$, $x$ is irrational
if and only if its digit sequence is not eventually periodic. The technical core is a toolkit
for prescribing decimal expansions: an exact head/tail decomposition, a digit-recovery theorem
valid precisely when the digit $9$ is excluded, a Liouville-type gap criterion for
irrationality, and a grafting operator that transplants an arbitrary tail onto an arbitrary
prefix.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} decimal expansion, normality, simple normality, digit frequency,
autocorrelation, lacunary series, irrationality criteria, Liouville argument, prefix
indeterminacy.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The folklore claim and what is wrong with it}

It is a commonplace that the decimal digits of $\pi$, of $e$ and of $\sqrt 2$ ``behave
randomly''. The commonplace is supported by computation: billions of digits have been produced
and subjected to frequency tests, block tests, gap tests and autocorrelation tests, and all of
them are passed. It is also supported, at a distance, by measure theory: almost every real
number is normal in every base.

Neither support gives a theorem about $\pi$. The measure-theoretic statement is about a
full-measure set to which no specific constant is known to belong. The computational statement
is about a finite word. The purpose of this paper is to show that the second gap is not merely
an unclosed one but a structurally unclosable one: the map
\[
\text{(first $n$ digits of $x$)} \longmapsto \text{(asymptotic digit statistics of $x$)}
\]
is not a function, and this can be witnessed by explicit, elementary, completely controlled
counterexamples, uniformly in $n$.

\subsection{What irrationality does and does not buy}

Irrationality is not vacuous for digits. The classical dichotomy---recalled and proved below as
Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy}---says that for $x \in [0,1)$, $x$ is irrational if and only if its
digit sequence is not eventually periodic. This is the exact digit content of irrationality: no
eventually repeating block. It is also \emph{all} of the digit content. Every asymptotic
frequency statement, every autocorrelation statement, every normality statement is logically
independent of it, as our witnesses demonstrate: two of our irrational numbers have
nonzero-digit densities $0$ and $1$ respectively, both maximally far from the value $9/10$
forced by simple normality.

\subsection{Method}

Everything rests on two operations. \emph{Prescription} builds a real number from a designed
digit sequence and guarantees that the design is what one gets
(Theorem~\ref{thm:recovery}). \emph{Grafting} replaces the tail of a number beyond position $n$
by a designed tail while leaving the first $n$ digits untouched (Section~\ref{sec:graft}). The
designs are \emph{lacunary}: the interesting digits sit on
\[
\Lambda = \{\, m \in \NN : m+1 \text{ is a power of } 2 \,\} = \{0,1,3,7,15,31,63,\dots\},
\]
a set with two contradictory-sounding virtues. It is \emph{sparse}, with
$\#(\Lambda \cap [0,M)) \le \log_2 M + 1$, so anything supported on it has density zero; and it
is \emph{spread}, with unbounded gaps, so anything supported on it defeats periodicity through
a Liouville estimate. That tension is the engine of the entire paper.

Throughout, for $x \ge 0$ we write
\[
d_m(x) = \big\lfloor x \cdot 10^{\,m+1} \big\rfloor \bmod 10, \qquad m \ge 0,
\]
for the $m$-th decimal digit of $x$ after the point, and $\log_2$ denotes the integer logarithm.

\section{Prescribing decimal expansions}

\subsection{Values of digit sequences}

\begin{definition}
For a sequence $d = (d_m)_{m\ge0}$ with $d_m \in \{0,\dots,9\}$ set
$\val(d) = \sum_{m\ge0} d_m 10^{-(m+1)} \in [0,1]$. The series converges absolutely.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Bounds]\label{lem:bounds}
If $d_m \le 8$ for all $m$ then $\val(d) \le 8/9 < 1$. If $d_j \ne 0$ for some $j$ then
$\val(d) > 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Termwise comparison with $\sum_m \tfrac{8}{10}\cdot 10^{-m} = \tfrac89$ gives the first claim;
the second follows since all terms are nonnegative and the $j$-th is strictly positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Head/tail decomposition]\label{lem:headtail}
Let $H_n(d)$ be the natural number with base-ten representation $d_0 d_1 \cdots d_{n-1}$,
defined by $H_0(d) = 0$ and $H_{n+1}(d) = 10 H_n(d) + d_n$. Then for all $n$,
\[
10^n \val(d) = H_n(d) + \val(\sigma^n d), \qquad
\sigma^n d := (d_n, d_{n+1}, d_{n+2},\dots).
\]
In particular, if $d_j = 0$ for all $j < L$ then $\val(d) = 10^{-L}\val(\sigma^L d)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Split the series at index $n$ and multiply by $10^n$; the tail reindexes to
$\val(\sigma^n d)$. For the head, induction on $n$ using
$10^{n+1}\sum_{i<n+1} d_i 10^{-(i+1)} = 10 \cdot 10^{n}\sum_{i<n} d_i 10^{-(i+1)} + d_n$
and the recursion for $H$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Digit recovery and the role of the digit nine}

\begin{theorem}[Digit Recovery]\label{thm:recovery}
Let $d$ be a digit sequence with $d_m \le 8$ for all $m$. Then $d_k(\val(d)) = d_k$ for all $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $k$ and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:headtail} with $n = k+1$:
$\val(d) \cdot 10^{k+1} = H_{k+1}(d) + T$ with $T = \val(\sigma^{k+1}d)$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:bounds}, $0 \le T \le 8/9 < 1$, and $H_{k+1}(d) \in \NN$, so
$\lfloor \val(d)10^{k+1}\rfloor = H_{k+1}(d) = 10H_k(d) + d_k$. Reduce modulo $10$ and use
$d_k < 10$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Necessity]
Some hypothesis excluding the digit $9$ is unavoidable: $d = (9,9,9,\dots)$ has $\val(d)=1$,
whose digits are all $0$. The collision $0.999\ldots = 1.000\ldots$ is the unique obstruction,
and forbidding $9$ is the cleanest hypothesis that kills it. All designs below use digits in
$\{0,1,2,3\}$.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Irrationality from gaps}

\begin{theorem}[Gap Criterion]\label{thm:gaps}
Let $d$ be a digit sequence with $d_m \le 8$ for all $m$. Suppose that for every $L$ there is a
$k$ with $d_j = 0$ for $k \le j < k+L$ and $d_j \ne 0$ for some $j \ge k+L$. Then $\val(d)$ is
irrational.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $\val(d) = p/q$ with $q \ge 1$. Apply the hypothesis with $L = q+1$, obtaining $k$, and
set $T = \val(\sigma^k d)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:headtail}, $T = 10^k \val(d) - H_k(d)$, so
$N := qT = 10^k p - q H_k(d)$ is an integer. Since some digit beyond $k$ is nonzero,
Lemma~\ref{lem:bounds} gives $T > 0$, hence $N \ge 1$. Since the first $L$ entries of
$\sigma^k d$ vanish, Lemma~\ref{lem:headtail} and Lemma~\ref{lem:bounds} give
$T = 10^{-L}\val(\sigma^{k+L}d) \le \tfrac89 10^{-L}$, and $q < 2^q \le 2^L \le 10^L$ yields
$N \le \tfrac89 q 10^{-L} < 1$. Contradiction.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Rationality from periodicity, and digitwise addition}

\begin{theorem}[Eventual periodicity implies rationality]\label{thm:periodic}
If $d_{i+p+n} = d_{i+n}$ for all $i$, with $p \ge 1$, then $\val(d) \in \QQ$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $e = \sigma^n d$, $u = \val(e)$. Periodicity means $\sigma^p e = e$, so
Lemma~\ref{lem:headtail} gives $10^p u = H_p(e) + u$, i.e.\ $u = H_p(e)/(10^p-1) \in \QQ$;
then $\val(d) = (H_n(d) + u)/10^n \in \QQ$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Digitwise additivity]\label{lem:add}
If $c$, $d$, $e$ are digit sequences with $e_m = c_m + d_m$ for all $m$ (no carrying), then
$\val(e) = \val(c) + \val(d)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
All three series converge absolutely and the summands add termwise.
\end{proof}

\section{Lacunary witnesses and the grafting operator}\label{sec:graft}

\subsection{The lacunary position set}

\begin{definition}
Call $m \in \NN$ \emph{lacunary} if $m+1$ is a power of two, and let
$\Lambda = \{0,1,3,7,15,31,63,\dots\}$ be the set of lacunary positions.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Counting bound]\label{lem:count}
For every $M \ge 1$, $\ \#(\Lambda \cap [0,M)) \le \log_2 M + 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The map $m \mapsto \log_2(m+1)$ is injective on $\Lambda$, since $2^{\log_2(m+1)} = m+1$ there,
and $m + 1 \le M$ forces $\log_2(m+1) \le \log_2 M$; so it embeds $\Lambda \cap [0,M)$ into
$\{0,\dots,\log_2 M\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Unbounded gaps]\label{lem:gaps}
Between $2^N - 1$ and $2^{N+1}-1$ there is no lacunary position, and lacunary positions occur
arbitrarily far out, at $2^M - 1$ for every $M$. Hence a sequence supported on $\Lambda$ has
runs of zeros of every length, each followed by a later nonzero digit.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Shifted logarithm]\label{lem:logshift}
For $K \ge 1$: $\log_2(K+1) \le \log_2 K + 1$, hence $\log_2(K+r) \le \log_2 K + r$ for all
$r \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$K+1 \le 2K$ and $\log_2(2K) = \log_2 K + 1$; then induct on $r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Density criteria]\label{lem:density}
Let $f : \NN \to \NN$ and $a,c \in \NN$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $f(M) \le a + c\log_2 M$ for all $M \ge 1$, then $f(M)/M \to 0$.
\item If $f(M) \le M$ for all $M$ and $M \le f(M) + a + c\log_2 M$ for all $M \ge 1$, then
$f(M)/M \to 1$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(1) squeeze against $c(\log_2 M)/M + a/M \to 0$. (2) apply (1) to $g(M) = M - f(M)$ and use
$f(M)/M = 1 - g(M)/M$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The three primary witnesses}

\begin{definition}\label{def:witnesses}
Define digit sequences and their values:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
name & digit at $m \in \Lambda$ & digit at $m \notin \Lambda$ & value \\
\midrule
sparse & $1$ & $0$ & $S = 0.1101000100000001000\ldots$\\
dense & $2$ & $1$ & $D = 0.2212111211111112111\ldots$\\
alternating & $2$ ($m$ even), $3$ ($m$ odd) & $1$ ($m$ even), $2$ ($m$ odd) &
$A = 0.2313121312121213121\ldots$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
All digits lie in $\{0,1,2,3\}$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:recovery} applies and the decimal digits of
each value are exactly the prescribed sequence.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:sparse}
$S$ is irrational, and the density of its nonzero digits is $0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Irrationality: Lemma~\ref{lem:gaps} supplies the hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:gaps}. Density:
the nonzero digits below $M$ are exactly $\Lambda \cap [0,M)$, of size at most
$\log_2 M + 1$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:count}, so Lemma~\ref{lem:density}(1) applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:dense}
$D = S + \tfrac19$ is irrational, all of its digits are nonzero, and its nonzero-digit density
is $1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The digit sequence of $D$ is that of $S$ plus the constant sequence $1$, of value
$\sum_m 10^{-(m+1)} = \tfrac19$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:add}. Irrationality follows since $S$ is
irrational and $\tfrac19$ rational.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:alt}
$A = \tfrac{4}{33} + S$, and $A$ is irrational.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The base pattern $1,2,1,2,\dots$ has period two, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:periodic} its value is
rational; explicitly $0.\overline{12} = 12/99 = 4/33$. The digit sequence of $A$ is the base
pattern plus the sparse sequence (each lacunary digit bumped by one), so Lemma~\ref{lem:add}
gives $A = \tfrac4{33} + S$, an irrational plus a rational.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Irrationality does not imply normality]\label{cor:notnormal}
Call $x$ \emph{simply normal in base ten} if for every digit $c$ the frequency
$\#\{m<M : d_m(x)=c\}/M$ tends to $\tfrac1{10}$; this forces nonzero-digit density $\tfrac9{10}$.
Hence neither $S$ (density $0$) nor $D$ (density $1$) is simply normal, although both are
irrational.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Grafting}

\begin{definition}
For $x \ge 0$, $n \in \NN$, $t \in [0,1)$, the \emph{graft} is
$G(x,n,t) = \big(\lfloor x 10^n\rfloor + t\big)/10^n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Graft properties]\label{thm:graft}
Let $x \ge 0$, $n \in \NN$, $t \in [0,1)$. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item $d_k(G(x,n,t)) = d_k(x)$ for every $k < n$;
\item $d_{n+m}(G(x,n,t)) = d_m(t)$ for every $m \ge 0$;
\item $G(x,n,t)$ is irrational if and only if $t$ is;
\item $|G(x,n,t) - x| < 10^{-n}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(1) Write $n = k+1+j$. Then
$G(x,n,t)10^{k+1} = (\lfloor x10^n\rfloor + t)/10^j$ and $x10^{k+1} = (x 10^n)/10^j$. Since
$0 \le t < 1$ we have $\lfloor \lfloor x10^n\rfloor + t\rfloor = \lfloor x10^n\rfloor$, and
$\lfloor \alpha/N\rfloor = \lfloor \lfloor\alpha\rfloor/N\rfloor$ for a positive integer $N$
makes both floors equal to the integer quotient $\lfloor x10^n\rfloor / 10^j$.

(2) $G(x,n,t)10^{n+m+1} = t\,10^{m+1} + \lfloor x10^n\rfloor 10^{m+1}$; the second summand is an
integer divisible by $10$, so taking floors and reducing modulo $10$ leaves $d_m(t)$.

(3) $G(x,n,t)$ is an integer translate of $t$ divided by the nonzero integer $10^n$.

(4) Both numbers lie in the interval
$[\lfloor x10^n\rfloor 10^{-n}, (\lfloor x10^n\rfloor+1)10^{-n})$ of length $10^{-n}$.
\end{proof}

Write $S_{x,n} = G(x,n,S)$, $D_{x,n} = G(x,n,D)$, $A_{x,n} = G(x,n,A)$, $R_{x,n} = G(x,n,0)$.

\section{Prefix indeterminacy for first-order statistics}

\begin{lemma}[Statistics of the grafts]\label{lem:graftstats}
For every $x \ge 0$ and $n, M \in \NN$ with $M \ge 1$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the number of nonzero digits of $S_{x,n}$ below $M$ is at most $n + \log_2 M + 1$;
\item the number of zero digits of $D_{x,n}$ below $M$ is at most $n$;
\item $R_{x,n} = \lfloor x 10^n\rfloor/10^n$ is rational, with all digits from position $n$ on
equal to zero.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:graft}(2) the digits at positions $m \ge n$ are the witness digits at
$m-n$; for $S$ these are nonzero only on $\Lambda$, of which at most $\log_2 M + 1$ lie below
$M$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:count}), and at most $n$ positions lie below $n$. For $D$ no tail digit is
zero, so zeros occur only among the $n$ prefix positions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No finite prefix determines any digit law]\label{thm:main1}
Let $x \ge 0$ and $n \in \NN$. Then there exist $y, z, w \in \RR$ with
$d_k(y) = d_k(z) = d_k(w) = d_k(x)$ for all $k < n$, such that
\begin{itemize}
\item $y$ is irrational and its nonzero-digit density is $0$;
\item $z$ is irrational and its nonzero-digit density is $1$;
\item $w$ is rational;
\item none of $y,z,w$ is simply normal in base ten;
\item $d_n(y) = 1$, $d_n(z) = 2$, $d_n(w) = 0$, so the three are pairwise distinct.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $y = S_{x,n}$, $z = D_{x,n}$, $w = R_{x,n}$. The prefix statement is
Theorem~\ref{thm:graft}(1); rationality and irrationality follow from
Theorem~\ref{thm:graft}(3) with Theorems~\ref{thm:sparse} and~\ref{thm:dense}. The densities
follow from Lemma~\ref{lem:graftstats} with Lemma~\ref{lem:density}: part (1) of the lemma with
$a = n+1$, $c=1$ gives density $0$ for $y$; the zero-count bound $n$ gives density $1$ for $z$.
Simple normality would force nonzero-digit density $9/10$
(Corollary~\ref{cor:notnormal}). Finally $0 \in \Lambda$, so the digit at position $n$ of each
graft is the $0$-th digit of the corresponding tail: $1$, $2$ and $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Metric form]\label{thm:metric}
Let $x \ge 0$ and $\varepsilon > 0$. Then there are an irrational, non-simply-normal $y$ and a
rational $w$ with $|y-x| < \varepsilon$ and $|w-x| < \varepsilon$, both agreeing with $x$ in as
long a prefix as desired.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose $n$ with $10^{-n} < \varepsilon$ and combine Theorem~\ref{thm:main1} with
Theorem~\ref{thm:graft}(4).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cardinal form]\label{thm:cardinal}
For every $x \ge 0$ and every $n$, the set
$\{ y \in \RR : d_k(y) = d_k(x) \text{ for } k<n,\ y \text{ irrational},\ y \text{ not simply
normal}\}$ is uncountable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For a bit stream $b \in \{0,1\}^{\NN}$ define the digit sequence $\beta_b$ by $\beta_b(m) = 1$ if
$m \in \Lambda$ and $b_{\log_2(m+1)} = 1$; $\beta_b(m) = 2$ if $m \in \Lambda$ and
$b_{\log_2(m+1)} = 0$; $\beta_b(m) = 0$ otherwise; and put $B(b) = \val(\beta_b)$. All digits
are at most $8$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:recovery} recovers them. Each $\beta_b$ has support
$\Lambda$, so $B(b)$ is irrational by Lemmas~\ref{lem:gaps} and Theorem~\ref{thm:gaps}, and has
nonzero-digit density $0$ by Lemmas~\ref{lem:count} and~\ref{lem:density}(1), hence is not simply
normal. Grafting preserves all of these (Theorem~\ref{thm:graft}) and $b \mapsto G(x,n,B(b))$ is
injective, because the stream is read back off the digits at positions $n + (2^i-1)$. An
injection of $\{0,1\}^{\NN}$ into the set exhibits it as of cardinality the continuum.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every $n$, each of $\sqrt 2$, $\pi$ and $e$ admits three numbers sharing its first $n$
decimal digits which are respectively irrational of nonzero-digit density $0$, irrational of
nonzero-digit density $1$, and rational, none simply normal; and continuum many irrational
non-simply-normal numbers share those $n$ digits.
\end{corollary}

\section{The positive side: irrationality is exactly aperiodicity}

\begin{theorem}[Periodicity Dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Let $x \in [0,1)$. Then $x$ is irrational if and only if its digit sequence is \emph{not}
eventually periodic, i.e.\ there are no $n \ge 0$ and $p \ge 1$ with $d_{m+p}(x) = d_m(x)$ for
all $m \ge n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) is Theorem~\ref{thm:periodic} in contrapositive form, using that reading the
digits of $x \in [0,1)$ and summing them back returns $x$.

($\Rightarrow$) If $x$ is rational with denominator $q$, then cutting at position $k$ replaces
$x$ by $\{10^k x\}$, in the sense that $d_{k+j}(x) = d_j(\{10^k x\})$ for all $j$. The orbit
$\{\{10^k x\} : k \ge 0\}$ consists of rationals in $[0,1)$ with denominator dividing $q$,
hence is finite; so $\{10^k x\} = \{10^{k'}x\}$ for some $k<k'$, and $p = k'-k$ is a period from
position $k$ on.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The sparse witness $S$ is irrational, hence its digit sequence is not eventually periodic, even
though its digits are zero in density one. Aperiodicity is compatible with total statistical
degeneracy.
\end{corollary}

\section{Second-order statistics: autocorrelation indeterminacy}

\begin{definition}
For $x \ge 0$ and $r,M \in \NN$ put
$A_r(x,M) = \#\{ m < M : d_m(x) = d_{m+r}(x)\}$, the \emph{lag-$r$ agreement count}. Say $x$ has
\emph{lag-$r$ agreement density} $\alpha$ if $A_r(x,M)/M \to \alpha$.
\end{definition}

For a heuristically random digit sequence one expects $\alpha = 1/10$ at every lag $r \ge 1$.
Note the complementarity $A_r(x,M) + \#\{m<M : d_m(x)\ne d_{m+r}(x)\} = M$, which converts an
upper bound on disagreements into a density-one statement via Lemma~\ref{lem:density}(2).

\begin{lemma}[Exceptional-set bound]\label{lem:exceptional}
Let $n,r,M \in \NN$ with $M \ge 1$ and let $E \subseteq [0,M)$ be finite with every $m \in E$
satisfying $m < n$, or $m-n \in \Lambda$, or $(m-n)+r \in \Lambda$. Then
\[
\#E \ \le\ n + (\log_2 M + 1) + (\log_2 M + r + 1).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Cover $E$ by the $n$ positions below $n$; the image of $\Lambda \cap [0,M)$ under
$p \mapsto n+p$, of size at most $\log_2 M + 1$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:count}); and the image of
$\Lambda \cap [0,M+r)$ under $p \mapsto n+p-r$, of size at most
$\log_2(M+r)+1 \le \log_2 M + r + 1$ (Lemmas~\ref{lem:count} and~\ref{lem:logshift}). Then use
subadditivity of cardinality under unions and images.
\end{proof}

The appearance of the second, \emph{shifted} copy of the lacunary set is exactly what
distinguishes the lag-$r$ argument from the frequency argument;
Lemma~\ref{lem:logshift} keeps the bound logarithmic in $M$ uniformly in the lag.

\begin{theorem}[The alternating graft]\label{thm:altgraft}
Let $x \ge 0$, $n \in \NN$ and $y = A_{x,n}$. Then $y$ is irrational, its lag-$1$ agreement
density is $0$, and its lag-$2$ agreement density is $1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Irrationality is Theorem~\ref{thm:alt} with Theorem~\ref{thm:graft}(3).

\emph{Lag $1$.} If $m \ge n$ and neither $m-n$ nor $(m-n)+1$ is lacunary, then by
Theorem~\ref{thm:graft}(2) the digits of $y$ at $m$ and $m+1$ are the base alternating digits at
$m-n$ and $(m-n)+1$, which are $1$ and $2$ in some order, hence different. So every agreement
position lies in the exceptional set of Lemma~\ref{lem:exceptional} with $r=1$, giving
$A_1(y,M) \le n + 4 + 2\log_2 M$; Lemma~\ref{lem:density}(1) yields density $0$.

\emph{Lag $2$.} If $m \ge n$ and neither $m-n$ nor $(m-n)+2$ is lacunary, the digits at $m$ and
$m+2$ are base digits at positions of the same parity, hence equal. So every disagreement
position lies in the exceptional set with $r=2$, of size at most $n + 5 + 2\log_2 M$, and
Lemma~\ref{lem:density}(2) yields agreement density $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The dense graft]\label{thm:densegraft}
Let $x \ge 0$, $n \in \NN$ and $z = D_{x,n}$. Then $z$ is irrational and for every lag $r \ge 0$
its lag-$r$ agreement density equals $1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $m \ge n$ and neither $m-n$ nor $(m-n)+r$ is lacunary, both digits equal $1$ and hence
agree. Disagreement positions therefore lie in the exceptional set of
Lemma~\ref{lem:exceptional}, of size at most $n + r + 3 + 2\log_2 M$; apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:density}(2).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No finite prefix determines an autocorrelation law]\label{thm:main2}
Let $x \ge 0$ and $n \in \NN$. Then there exist irrational $y$ and $z$ with
$d_k(y) = d_k(z) = d_k(x)$ for all $k<n$ such that $y$ has lag-$1$ agreement density $0$ and
lag-$2$ agreement density $1$, while $z$ has agreement density $1$ at every lag. Consequently
neither the value of the autocorrelation at a given lag, nor its functional dependence on the
lag, is determined by any finite decimal prefix.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $y = A_{x,n}$, $z = D_{x,n}$ and combine
Theorems~\ref{thm:graft},~\ref{thm:altgraft},~\ref{thm:densegraft}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every $n$ there are irrational numbers sharing the first $n$ decimal digits of $\sqrt 2$
whose autocorrelation profiles are, respectively, the strongly lag-dependent profile
$(0,1,\dots)$ and the constant profile $1$. The same holds for $\pi$ and $e$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Lacunary digit generation.} Given a pattern rule and a bound $M$, emit the first $M$
digits of a witness. Membership in $\Lambda$ is tested by the bit identity ``$m+1$ is a power of
two iff $(m+1)\ \&\ m = 0$'', so the sequence is produced in $O(M)$ integer operations and
$O(1)$ working memory.

\textbf{Exact grafting.} Given $x$ (rational or high-precision), a length $n$ and a witness rule,
output the grafted number as the exact fraction
$(\lfloor x10^n\rfloor 10^{K} + \text{tail}_K)/10^{\,n+K}$ truncated at $K$ tail digits, with
certified error $10^{-(n+K)}$. Cost $O(n+K)$ big-integer digit operations.

\textbf{Statistic evaluation.} Given a digit array of length $M$, compute the nonzero-digit
count, the ten digit frequencies and the lag-$r$ agreement counts for $r$ in a range $R$ in
$O(M|R|)$ time. Comparing against the certified bounds $n + \log_2 M + 1$ and
$n + r + 3 + 2\log_2 M$ turns the theorems into checkable numerical statements at each finite
$M$.

\textbf{Bit-stream witness.} Given a finite bit prefix $b_0,\dots,b_{k-1}$, emit the digits of
$B(b)$: digit $1$ or $2$ at position $2^i-1$ according to $b_i$, digit $0$ elsewhere. This makes
the injection of Theorem~\ref{thm:cardinal} constructive.

\section{Numerical illustration}

For the sparse witness the nonzero-digit count and the certified bound $\log_2 M + 1$ agree
exactly at the scales tested:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
$M$ & $10$ & $100$ & $1000$ & $10000$\\
\midrule
nonzero count & $4$ & $7$ & $10$ & $14$\\
bound $\log_2 M + 1$ & $4$ & $7$ & $10$ & $14$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

For the alternating witness the lag-$1$ agreement count is $0$ at $M=100$ and at $M=1000$---the
anticorrelation is total, not merely asymptotic---while the lag-$2$ counts are $90$ and $984$,
i.e.\ ratios $0.900$ and $0.984$ climbing to $1$. For the dense witness the lag-$1$, lag-$3$ and
lag-$7$ counts at $M=1000$ are $983$, $984$, $985$: density $1$ at every lag, the deficits being
exactly the logarithmically many lacunary collisions.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the theorems do and do not say}
They do \emph{not} say that $\pi$ is abnormal. Almost every real is normal, $\pi$ is conjectured
to be, and every computation is consistent with it. They say that the computation cannot be the
ground of the belief: the inference ``the first $N$ digits are equidistributed, therefore the
digits are equidistributed'' fails catastrophically, since the conclusion can be replaced by its
extreme negation while the premise is held fixed, for every $N$, by uncountably many witnesses
arbitrarily close to the original number.

\subsection{Why normality proofs are hard, structurally}
Theorem~\ref{thm:main1} explains the shape of the difficulty. A proof that $\sqrt 2$ is normal
must use a property of $\sqrt 2$ that is not a property of its truncations---its algebraicity,
the equation $y^2 = 2$, the dynamics of $\{10^k\sqrt 2\}$, or a Diophantine input. Any argument
whose only input is the computed prefix is provably insufficient, since the grafts satisfy that
input and violate the conclusion.

\subsection{The role of lacunarity}
The set $\Lambda$ sits precisely in the gap between the two notions. It is
\emph{density-invisible}, with counting function $O(\log M)$, so modifications supported on it
change no asymptotic frequency; and \emph{periodicity-lethal}, with geometrically growing gaps,
so the Liouville estimate forbids the tail from ever being rational. The same perturbation that
guarantees irrationality is invisible to every density statistic. It is also sharp in the
observed data: the sparse witness attains the bound $\log_2 M + 1$ exactly at
$M = 10,10^2,10^3,10^4$.

\subsection{The autocorrelation half}
The autocorrelation results are not a formal restatement of the frequency results. The counting
genuinely changes: the exceptional positions for lag $r$ come from two shifted copies of
$\Lambda$, requiring $\log_2(M+r)\le \log_2 M + r$. Moreover the alternating witness shows that
a single number can exhibit radically different densities at different lags---$0$ at lag $1$,
$1$ at lag $2$---so no scalar summarises the correlation structure, and an experimenter
measuring only lag $1$ would draw the opposite conclusion from one measuring lag $2$.

\subsection{Relation to the classical picture}
Nothing here contradicts Borel's theorem, nor the known explicit normal numbers such as
Champernowne's constant $0.123456789101112\ldots$. Those constructions determine their
statistics by prescribing the \emph{entire} expansion. Our point is dual: prescribing a
\emph{finite} portion determines nothing, and the space of continuations is, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:cardinal}, of full cardinality and full statistical variety.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{Sub-logarithmic rigidity.} Our witnesses are extremal: the nonzero-digit count of the
sparse witness equals $\log_2 M + 1$ exactly at $M = 10,10^2,10^3,10^4$. Conjecture: this is
forced---for any real whose digit support has counting function $o(\log M)$ the number is
rational. The insight is that a support sparser than logarithmic forces gaps so long that the
Liouville-type tail estimate degenerates into a transcendence estimate, so the number becomes a
Liouville number rather than merely irrational: the dividing line between ``irrational'' and
``transcendental'' for lacunary expansions is a growth condition on the support, not an
arithmetic one.

\textbf{Prescribed frequency vector at a prescribed prefix.} We realise nonzero-digit densities
$0$ and $1$. Conjecture: for every prefix of every $x \ge 0$ and every probability vector
$(p_0,\dots,p_9)$ with rational entries there is an irrational number with that prefix whose
digit-$c$ frequency is exactly $p_c$. The insight is that a Champernowne-style block
construction spliced onto the graft and perturbed at lacunary positions decouples ``which
frequencies are attained'' (a block-length computation) from ``is the number irrational'' (a gap
condition supplied for free by the perturbation).

\textbf{Full autocorrelation spectrum realisation.} We produce a number with lag-$1$
autocorrelation $0$ and lag-$2$ autocorrelation $1$. Conjecture: every profile
$A : \NN_{\ge1}\to\{0,1\}$ realisable by a periodic pattern is realisable by an irrational
number with any prescribed prefix, and the realisable profiles are exactly the profiles of
finite words.

\textbf{Beyond base ten.} Every argument is base-independent after replacing ``digit $\le 8$''
by ``digit $\le b-2$''; a uniform treatment over all bases, and the interaction of prefixes in
different bases, is a natural next step.

\textbf{Quantitative indeterminacy.} Our proofs give explicit thresholds: $n + O(\log M)$
exceptional positions, so the influence of a length-$n$ prefix on any density decays like
$n/M$. Making this sharp would quantify exactly how far one must look before two witnesses
become statistically distinguishable.

\section{Conclusion}

The decimal expansion of a real number carries exactly one piece of information about its
arithmetic nature---whether the expansion is eventually periodic---and that piece is invisible
to every density statistic. Conversely, every density statistic is invisible to every finite
prefix. Formally: for each $x \ge 0$ and each $n$, the fibre of the ``first $n$ digits'' map
contains rationals, irrationals with nonzero-digit density $0$, irrationals with nonzero-digit
density $1$, irrationals whose digit autocorrelation vanishes at lag $1$ and is total at lag
$2$, irrationals whose autocorrelation is total at every lag, and continuum many pairwise
distinct irrational non-simply-normal numbers besides. Whatever a computation of the first $n$
digits of $\pi$ reveals, it reveals it about those $n$ digits.

